Reload DS/ES/SS at the 32-bit entry, for a bootloader that sets only CS.

The 32-bit boot protocol says the loader must hand the kernel flat 4 GB
__BOOT_CS (0x10) and __BOOT_DS (0x18) descriptors and have DS/ES/SS already
loaded with the data one. Until v5.x the kernel did not rely on that: it
reloaded DS/ES/SS from __BOOT_DS itself unless the loader set KEEP_SEGMENTS.
That code went away with KEEP_SEGMENTS, and startup_32 now takes the loader at
its word -- the `call 1f` three instructions in is a push through whatever SS
the loader left behind.

The Cisco Nexus 3172TQ's bootloader does not set one. It is a customised GNU
GRUB 2 built as a UEFI application, and its handoff trampoline -- 176 bytes
memcpy'd to physical 0x700 -- does this and nothing else:

    cli; cld
    lgdt <its own GDT>; lidt <null IDT>
    ljmp $0x10, $32bit_entry        <- loads CS, and only CS
    clear CR0.PG; clear EFER.LME; CR4 = 0
    jmp *%ebx                       <- boot_params->hdr.code32_start

Its GDT is correct and complete -- 0x10 is a flat 32-bit code segment and 0x18
a flat 32-bit data segment, exactly as the protocol requires -- but it never
writes DS, ES or SS, so they still hold the selectors UEFI was running with.
The far jump fixes CS. Nothing fixes the rest.

So the kernel reaches

    cld
    cli
    leal    (BP_scratch+4)(%esi), %esp
    call    1f

and that call pushes a return address through a stale SS. On this board that
triple-faults and the box resets, with no output at all -- not even
earlyprintk, because nothing has run yet. The vendor's own 3.4 kernel boots on
the same loader because it still had the reload:

    testb   $(1<<6), BP_loadflags(%esi)      /* KEEP_SEGMENTS */
    jne     1f
    cli
    movl    $(__BOOT_DS), %eax
    movl    %eax, %ds
    movl    %eax, %es
    movl    %eax, %ss
 1:

Doing it again here is idempotent on a conforming loader: 0x18 is the
selector the protocol mandates, so on a loader that already set DS/ES/SS we
reload them with the value they already have. It costs four instructions
before the first memory access.

This is deliberately NOT conditional on KEEP_SEGMENTS. That flag meant "the
loader has set up segments it wants kept", and a loader that sets only CS is
not making that claim -- it is simply older than the assumption the kernel now
makes.

Only the 32-bit legacy entry is touched. The EFI stub and the 64-bit entry
come in elsewhere and are unaffected.

--- a/arch/x86/boot/compressed/head_64.S
+++ b/arch/x86/boot/compressed/head_64.S
@@ -89,6 +89,21 @@ SYM_FUNC_START(startup_32)
 	cld
 	cli
 
+/*
+ * Reload the data segments before touching memory.
+ *
+ * The 32-bit boot protocol requires the loader to leave DS/ES/SS holding a
+ * flat 4 GB __BOOT_DS, and some do not -- a loader whose handoff sets CS with
+ * a far jump and nothing else leaves whatever the firmware was running with.
+ * The call below pushes through SS, so a stale one faults here, before any
+ * console exists to say so. Reloading is idempotent where the loader did its
+ * job, and is what the kernel itself did until KEEP_SEGMENTS was removed.
+ */
+	movl	$(__BOOT_DS), %eax
+	movl	%eax, %ds
+	movl	%eax, %es
+	movl	%eax, %ss
+
 /*
  * Calculate the delta between where we were compiled to run
  * at and where we were actually loaded at.  This can only be done
